{
  "about": [
    "ONE FIXTURE, TWO ENGINES (phone ordering plan 3.2 step 3 and graft 3). Each case is a create body and what each engine must answer for its SHAPE, so the TypeScript schema and place_order_request cannot drift.",
    "Core (packages/core/src/orders/place-order.parity.test.ts) reads each body with parseOrderCreateRequest and checks `core`.",
    "pgTAP (supabase/tests/<M1>_place_order_request.test.sql, PO-2) gets a generated block (scripts/gen-place-order-parity-sql.mjs): per case, the p_request the service would build from the body, the key, and `sql`. The block is printable ASCII: every other character in a request is written as a JSON \\uXXXX escape, which jsonb reads back to the same value. place_order_request must raise exactly that for a refused case and write nothing, or get past step 3 for an accepted one.",
    "Bodies: `body` patches the base body key by key (a value of \"$delete\" removes the key). The generator puts idempotencyKey (one per case), placerUserId (ids.placer) and warehouseId (ids.warehouse) first. A line {\"item\": k} is item k (ids.itemPrefix + k as 12 hex digits); {\"itemId\": ...} is used as written. `lines` may be {\"repeat\": n, \"quantity\": q} for n distinct items, or {\"repeat\": n, \"quantity\": q, \"item\": k} for n lines of item k. A string may be {\"$repeat\": \"x\", \"times\": n, \"suffix\": \"...\"}. \"$site\", \"$bundle\" and \"$other\" are ids.site, ids.bundle and ids.other.",
    "The database side: `sqlPatch` patches the generated p_request (snake_case) for a case only the database can see, and `sqlKey` replaces the key (null for none). The service passes the body's values through (kits stay out: they are the audit note only), surface 'app' unless patched. The parity organization's zone is UTC, so needed_by is the wall clock with Z; converting in a real zone is the service's (core wallClockToInstant), and whether it is still to come is step 8, not shape.",
    "core: \"accept\" or {\"reason\", \"field\"?} (parseOrderCreateRequest's refusal). sql: \"accept\" (gets past step 3: no 22023 is raised), {\"sqlstate\": \"22023\", \"hint\": \"order_invalid\" | \"delivery_needs_site\" | \"idempotency_key_required\" | \"idempotency_key_invalid\", \"field\"?} or {\"sqlstate\": \"42501\", \"hint\": \"placer_mismatch\"} (step 1), or \"unreachable\": core refuses the body and the service never calls the database, so there is nothing to check there.",
    "Both engines: trimming removes exactly the characters JavaScript's String.prototype.trim removes, no fewer and no more (A13 pads a name, an email and notes with every one of them; R44 and R45 end an email with U+0085 and U+200B, which it keeps); lengths are characters (code points), as Postgres length() counts them; the line cap counts lines as sent, before lines of the same item are summed (R41); each line's quantity must be a whole number of at least 1 BEFORE lines of the same item are summed, then each item's sum is at most 10,000 and the total at most 10,000 (per item first, then the total); a quantity past the database's integer range (R42, R43) or a line that is not an object (D05) is the same 22023 order_invalid, never a cast error; email: the rule the web applies today (zod 3's .email() after trimming), /^(?!\\.)(?!.*\\.\\.)([A-Z0-9_'+\\-\\.]*)[A-Z0-9_+-]@([A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9\\-]*\\.)+[A-Z]{2,}$/i, which Postgres AREs take as written with ~*, at most 254 characters; the on-behalf name and email come both or neither; a pickup's site: core refuses it, the database drops it (R21).",
    "After editing this file run: node scripts/gen-place-order-parity-sql.mjs (then --check passes, and the core parity test checks the block)."
  ],
